<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Mario Pagano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Mario Pagano was a Neapolitan jurist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> philosopher, author of the Saggi politici. A leader of the Parthenopean Republic of 1799, he drafted its constitution, which drew on French models but added an assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> to guard it. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy was restored that summer he was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> with other republican leaders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-mario-pagano.jpg" alt="Francesco Mario Pagano" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parmiani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mario_Pagano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francesco Mario Pagano was born in Brienza, in the southern Province of Potenza, and studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-naples-federico-ii">University of Naples Federico II</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-genovesi">Antonio Genovesi</a>. At the age of twenty he became special lecturer in moral philosophy there while practising law. He was a friend of Gaetano Filangieri, entered freemasonry with him and was elected venerable master of the Neapolitan lodge La philantropia. In the 1790s he did more than any other intellectual to give a political form to the reform tradition of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Pagano's Saggi politici (1783-85) gave a philosophical history of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, argued against torture and capital punishment and advocated milder penal codes. His Considerazioni sul processo criminale (1787) brought him international popularity and was praised by Le Moniteur Universel, the main newspaper of revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His other works included Sul processo criminale, the Esame politico dell'intera legislazione romana and the Discorso sull'origine e natura della poesia. He also translated works from Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and wrote six tragedies and one comedy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1794 Pagano defended Vincenzo Galiani, Vincenzo Vitaliani and Emanuele De Deo, three alleged conspirators against Ferdinand IV. After they were sentenced to death he lost his professorship, was arrested, imprisoned and expelled from the kingdom. He fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1796 and returned in 1799, when he drafted the constitution of the short-lived Neapolitan Republic. It resembled the French Constitution of 1793 but added a body of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a>, an authority to oversee the maintenance of the law, which is considered a precursor of the modern constitutional court.</p><p>After the fall of the republic Pagano was arrested and imprisoned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo">Castel Nuovo</a>. He was hanged in the Piazza del Mercato in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 29 October 1799, together with other revolutionaries including Domenico Cirillo, Giorgio Pigliacelli and Ignazio Ciaia. He is regarded as the founder of the Neapolitan school of law and as one of the most influential thinkers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, a moderate reformist seen as a forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francesco Mario Pagano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was led by intellectuals and liberal nobles such as Carlo Lauberg and the jurist <b>Francesco Mario Pagano</b>, who drafted a constitution.</blockquote><a href="/state/parthenopean-republic">Parthenopean Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brienza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.48,15.63&amp;year=1781" aria-label="Brienza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francesco Mario Pagano"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4075 4051h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4075 4051h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.48,15.63&amp;year=1781">What was here in 1781? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
